      "id": 1,
      "text": "Vineland was named after the Viking colony of Vinland in the late 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vineland%2C_Minnesota"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Vineland serves as the administrative center for the Mille Lacs Band of Ojibwe.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vineland%2C_Minnesota"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In Ojibwe, Vineland is called Neyaashiing, meaning 'on the point of land' because it sits on Indian Point jutting into Mille Lake.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vineland%2C_Minnesota"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A post office operated in Vineland from 1891 until 1921, helping residents send and receive mail for about thirty years.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vineland%2C_Minnesota"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Vineland’s western edge borders Crow Wing County while its eastern edge hugs the shore of Mille Lake.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vineland%2C_Minnesota"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Vineland lies in the northwestern part of Mille Lacs County, within the northern area of Kathio Township.",
